---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="OAuth">
  <h1>OAuth</h1>
  <p>
    Identity supports Google, Microsoft, and GitHub as upstream OAuth
    providers. The flow is server-owned: the client calls
    <code>BeginOAuthLogin</code>, redirects the browser to the returned
    provider URL, then completes the callback with <code>OAuthLogin</code>.
    Identity does the provider token exchange, verifies the upstream
    identity, upserts the local user, and issues its own access and
    refresh tokens.
  </p>

  <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>Security model.</strong> <code>BeginOAuthLogin</code> mints a
    signed state token plus a PKCE verifier. <code>OAuthLogin</code>
    rejects callbacks whose returned <code>state</code> does not match the
    server-minted token, so CSRF protection does not depend on a
    client-side state cache.
  </div>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>Configure each provider you intend to support:</p>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=common`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Start RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message BeginOAuthLoginRequest {
  string redirect_uri = 1;
  string provider     = 2;  // "google", "microsoft", or "github"
  string tenant       = 3;  // Microsoft tenant ID (optional)
}

message BeginOAuthLoginResponse {
  string authorization_url = 1;
  string state             = 2;
  string state_token       = 3;
  string code_verifier     = 4;
  int32 expires_in         = 5;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Callback RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message OAuthLoginRequest {
  string code          = 1;  // authorization code from provider
  string redirect_uri  = 2;
  string provider      = 3;  // "google", "microsoft", or "github"
  string code_verifier = 4;  // optional when state_token is present
  string tenant        = 5;  // Microsoft tenant ID (optional)
  string state         = 6;  // callback state from the provider redirect
  string state_token   = 7;  // opaque token minted by BeginOAuthLogin
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Response</h2>
  <Code code={`message OAuthLoginResponse {
  User user             = 1;
  string access_token   = 2;  // backend-issued JWT
  string refresh_token  = 4;
  int32 expires_in      = 5;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Upsert semantics</h2>
  <p>
    On a successful OAuth verification, Identity resolves the user by
    <code>(provider, provider_user_id)</code> first, then falls back to the
    provider email for first-time linking:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, active.</strong> Tokens are issued; profile
      fields (<code>name</code>, <code>avatar_url</code>) are updated from
      the OAuth claims.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, invited.</strong> The login is rejected with
      <code>invitation_pending</code> until they accept the invitation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, deactivated/suspended.</strong> Rejected with
      <code>account_not_active</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>New user.</strong> Created with status
      <code>USER_STATUS_ACTIVE</code> and role <code>member</code> (or
      <code>admin</code> if they are the first user in the deployment).
      No password hash is stored — subsequent <code>PasswordLogin</code>
      attempts return <code>no password set for this account</code> until
      they set one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>OAuth + password coexistence</h2>
  <p>
    A user created via OAuth has no password hash, but can opt in by going
    through <code>RequestPasswordReset</code> → email link →
    <code>AcceptInvitation</code>-style setup, or by an admin's
    <code>ResetUserPassword</code>. After that they can log in with either
    provider.
  </p>

  <h2>Duplicate-link safety: composite uniqueness</h2>
  <p>
    The link between a local user and a provider-side identity is stored
    as an <code>OAuthIdentity</code> row keyed on
    <code>(provider, provider_user_id)</code>. That tuple is declared
    <code>composite_unique</code> in <code>schema.proto</code>, so the
    storage layer enforces "at most one local user per provider-side
    identity" atomically — two concurrent <code>OAuthLogin</code> calls
    that race to link the same Google account onto two different local
    accounts will collide on a real unique index in the datastore
    (Postgres or SQLite), and exactly one of them succeeds. The repository
    keeps a pre-query to surface the friendly
    <code>composite unique violated (google, &lt;sub&gt;)</code> error
    message, but the pre-query is not how the invariant is enforced.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosted OAuth flow (Firebase-style)</h2>
  <p>
    On top of the headless RPCs above, identity also exposes a fully
    server-owned OAuth flow that handles the provider redirects and
    callbacks for you. Two plain HTTP routes (not Connect-RPC) drive
    it — the browser is 302-redirected through them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>GET /oauth/start/{`{provider}`}?return_to=...</code> — mints
      the state token, builds the provider authorization URL, and
      redirects the browser to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GET /oauth/callback/{`{provider}`}</code> — receives the
      provider's authorization code, exchanges it through the same
      <code>OAuthLogin</code> internals, mints an opaque single-use code
      (<code>OAuthOneTimeCode</code>, 60s TTL), and 302-redirects to
      <code>return_to?code=&lt;otc&gt;</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The SPA then exchanges the one-time code for tokens via the
    <code>RedeemOAuthCode</code> RPC. No token material is persisted at
    rest — only the user id is stored alongside the
    <code>code_hash</code>, so a leaked code store is useless after the
    60s window or a single redeem.
  </p>

  <h3>Enabling the hosted flow</h3>
  <p>
    The hosted flow is <strong>off by default</strong>. Enable it by
    setting an allowlist of <code>return_to</code> origins / prefixes:
  </p>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS=https://app.example.com,https://staging.example.com`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    A <code>return_to</code> must equal or be prefixed by an allowlist
    entry; anything else is rejected with HTTP 400 at
    <code>/oauth/start</code> before any provider round-trip. Empty (the
    default) disables the routes entirely — they return 404 and only the
    headless RPCs work. The single provider-facing redirect URI registered
    with each provider is
    <code>&lt;identity-origin&gt;/oauth/callback/&lt;provider&gt;</code>,
    so you configure it once per provider and reuse it across all your
    SPA origins (which appear only in the <code>return_to</code>
    allowlist, not at the provider).
  </p>

  <h3>End-to-end browser walkthrough</h3>
  <Code code={`// 1. SPA kicks off the flow by sending the user to /oauth/start.
//    No state-token round-trip on the client; identity owns it.
window.location.href =
  "https://auth.example.com/oauth/start/google" +
  "?return_to=" + encodeURIComponent("https://app.example.com/oauth/done");

// 2. Provider redirects back to /oauth/callback/google with ?code=...
//    Identity exchanges, links/creates the OAuthIdentity row, mints
//    a one-time code, and redirects to return_to with ?code=otc_...

// 3. /oauth/done in the SPA: exchange the otc for real tokens.
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const otc = params.get("code");
const { user, accessToken, refreshToken } = await client.redeemOAuthCode({
  code: otc,
});

storeTokens(accessToken, refreshToken);
window.location.replace("/dashboard");`} lang="typescript" />
  <p>
    See decision log §10 in <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/blob/main/docs/IDENTITY.md"><code>docs/IDENTITY.md</code></a>
    for the design rationale (why a one-time code rather than a
    fragment-token or cookie, why <code>return_to</code> is a separate
    signed artifact rather than a state-token claim, and why the
    allowlist is fail-closed).
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
